<!-- Website teaching layer for android/compose/recomposition-and-state.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Checked against developer.android.com (State and Jetpack Compose; strong skipping) on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A counter button shows "Count is 0". The developer taps it and the log says the count went up, but the label never changes. They add <code>remember</code>; still nothing. They create observable state without <code>remember</code>; it updates, but an unrelated rerun of its creating scope resets it. Three attempts, three different bugs, one missing mental model.</p>

In Compose, the screen is the result of running composable functions with the current state. Nothing redraws because a variable changed; it redraws because an **observable** state object that a composable **read** was written, which invalidates that composable so it runs again. This chapter builds that model from the counter up, then uses it to explain hoisting, list updates and infinite loops.

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: animation -->
### Tap the button three ways

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Which counter updates the label?</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:3"><div class="node" data-el="plain" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>var count = 0</code></strong><small data-text="plainV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="rem" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>var count2 = remember { 0 }</code></strong><small data-text="remV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="state" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>var count3 by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }</code></strong><small data-text="stateV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"First composition","caption":"All three show 0. Only count3 is a snapshot state object, and the Text that reads it registers a dependency on it.","add":["plain","rem","state"],"state":{"plain":"","rem":"","state":"done"},"text":{"plainV":"label: 0","remV":"label: 0","stateV":"label: 0 · read observed"}},
{"label":"Tap","caption":"Each click handler increments its variable. Only the write to count3 is a snapshot write, so only it invalidates the scope that read it.","state":{"plain":"warn","rem":"warn","state":"active"},"text":{"plainV":"local now 1 · nothing scheduled","remV":"local now 1 · nothing scheduled","stateV":"value 1 · scope invalidated"}},
{"label":"Recompose","caption":"The Button content scope that read count3 runs again and reads 1. The other two labels stay at 0 because their ordinary-variable writes did not invalidate a reader; Counter need not run again.","state":{"plain":"error","rem":"error","state":"done"},"text":{"plainV":"label: 0 (no invalidation)","remV":"label: 0 (no invalidation)","stateV":"label: 1"}},
{"label":"The fourth bug","caption":"Without remember, a state holder created in Counter can still update its reading Button content. If Counter itself later recomposes, it creates a new holder at 0. The reset depends on which scope runs again.","state":{"state":"warn"},"text":{"stateV":"without remember: resets when Counter re-runs"}}
]</script><figcaption>Observation needs both halves: <code>remember</code> keeps the same holder, and <code>mutableStateOf</code> makes its writes visible.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @diagram 173316bf913dc7c2 -->
### State down, events up

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-stack"><div class="node is-key"><strong>ViewModel: <code>StateFlow</code></strong><small>the screen's source of truth</small></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>state ↓</strong><small><code>collectAsState()</code> in the screen composable</small></div><div class="node"><strong>event ↑</strong><small>the click becomes an intent the ViewModel handles</small></div></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>Screen composable</strong><small>passes values down</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Button composable</strong><small>stateless: renders inputs, reports clicks</small></div></div></div><figcaption>The button never changes state itself; it asks the owner, and the new state flows back down.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Failure Modes :: check -->
**Will the UI update?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary><code>val items = remember { mutableStateOf(arrayListOf("A")) }; items.value.add("B")</code></summary>

No. The `ArrayList` changed in place, but the state's value was never reassigned, so no write was observed. Assign a new list, or use `mutableStateListOf`.
</details>

<details><summary><code>var shown by remember { mutableStateOf(false) }; shown = true</code> directly in the composable body.</summary>

It updates, but writing state during composition invalidates the composable that is running. Here it settles because the value stops changing; an increment would loop forever. Drive writes from events or effects.
</details>

<details><summary>A child composable receives <code>title: String</code> and the parent recomposes with the same title.</summary>

The child can be skipped: `String` is stable and compared with `equals`, so an equal value lets Compose keep the existing subtree.
</details>

